## Scenario context

Java 8 introduced the most impactful set of language features in Java's history — lambdas eliminated anonymous inner classes, streams replaced imperative loops, and Optional made null handling explicit. Java 9's module system drew a boundary around the JDK itself. Java 10's `var` removed redundant type declarations. Java 11 LTS added unicode-aware string methods, a built-in HTTP/2 client, and became the migration ba…

This walkthrough focuses on **Deep Dive: How Lambdas Are Compiled and Why Streams Are Lazy**.

Before making a design choice, clarify:

- Internals: Lambda Translation via invokedynamic Lambdas are not compiled to anonymous inner classes.
- Java 8 introduced a new JVM instruction — invokedynamic — specifically to support lambda translation.
- When the compiler encounters a lambda, it generates a CONSTANT InvokeDynamic call-site that defers the binding of the lambda's implementation to runtime.

**Concepts in play:** jvm-internals-and-garbage-collection, database-indexing, design-patterns-gof, tree-data-structures

## Stage 1 — The Boilerplate Era: What Java 7 and Earlier Demanded From You

- Before Java 8, creating a background task meant this: Five lines.
- The actual logic — System.out.println — is buried in boilerplate that exists purely because Java required a named type to represent a function.
- Similarly, filtering a list of users required a for loop, a mutable temporary list, a conditional, and a sort call: And checking for null meant either a chain of null guards or a crash at runtime: These were not isolate…
- Java 8 (March 2014) set out to fix all three categories at once.

*Applies **jvm-internals-and-garbage-collection** to the decision above.*

```java
// Java 7 — anonymous inner class just to run one line
Runnable r = new Runnable() {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Running in background");
    }
};
new Thread(r).start();
```

```java
// Java 7 — imperative, stateful, four moving parts
List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
for (User user : users) {
    if (user.isActive() && user.getAge() > 30) {
        result.add(user.getName());
    }
}
Collections.sort(result);
```

**Observable outcome:** Java 8 (March 2014) set out to fix all three categories at once.

## Stage 2 — Lambda Expressions and Functional Interfaces: Functions Finally Become First-Class

- A lambda expression is a concise way to represent a function that can be passed as a value.
- It works against any functional interface — any interface with exactly one abstract method.
- The @FunctionalInterface annotation documents the single-method contract and lets the compiler enforce it.
- You can create your own functional interfaces: Java 8 ships four core functional interfaces that cover the majority of use cases: Method references ( String::toUpperCase , this::process , ArrayList::new ) are syntactic…

*Applies **database-indexing** to the decision above.*

```java
// Java 7 — anonymous Comparator (7 lines for one comparison)
List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Charlie", "Alice", "Bob");
Collections.sort(names, new Comparator<String>() {
    @Override
    public int compare(String a, String b) {
        return a.compareTo(b);
    }
});
```

```java
// Java 8+ — lambda (one line)
List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Charlie", "Alice", "Bob");
names.sort((a, b) -> a.compareTo(b));

// Or even shorter — method reference
names.sort(String::compareTo);
```

