## Stage 3 — Stream API, Optional, and the Date/Time Overhaul

- The Stream Pipeline: Declarative Data Processing Streams transform collection processing from imperative (how to iterate) to declarative (what to select and transform): Streams are lazy : intermediate operations ( filte…
- The terminal operation ( collect , forEach , reduce , count ) triggers actual execution.
- This means a stream over a million-element list that filter s down to 10 elements and findFirst() terminates after finding the first match — it does not process all million elements first.
- Optional: Making Absence Explicit at the Type Level Optional<T wraps a value that may or may not be present.

*Applies **design-patterns-gof** to the decision above.*

```java
// Java 7 — imperative, mutable, four moving parts
List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
for (User user : users) {
    if (user.isActive() && user.getAge() > 30) {
        result.add(user.getName());
    }
}
Collections.sort(result);
```

```java
// Java 8+ — Stream pipeline
List<String> result = users.stream()
    .filter(u -> u.isActive() && u.getAge() > 30)
    .map(User::getName)
    .sorted()
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

**Observable outcome:** Optional: Making Absence Explicit at the Type Level Optional<T wraps a value that may or may not be present.

